date: 2026-08-17T00:00:00-04:00
---

Aut is a humanoid with completely white skin. He has oversized hands and feet, but unnaturally gangly, thin arms and legs. His torso is a simple cylinder. His head is perfectly spherical, overly large, and has absolutely no facial features except for a giant toothy grin that stretches from one side of his face to the other.

He makes a habit of observing the world, and nudging it in the direction he prefers through his "friends." The recent advent of the [[Voidsong]] has somehow disrupted his ability, however, much to his chagrin. He lives somewhere within the [[Geography/Black and White/index|Black and White]].

He can be met in the empty space between [[Færrin]] and the [[Godhome]], and in the white void above Save Your Soul Incorporated, reached by the elevator's three-dot button. When people of Færrin die, their souls enter the [[Pharos]] and float up to the Godhome, and he says this is the only way there. When Johnny Skyfall and Benny died at the [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Sableclutch/The Crowded Flea|Crowded Flea]], he pushed them back to where they had died, and later called back the pieces of themselves they had left in the Pharos; both are whole again. He set his mark on them, three blue dots in a triangle, which they can bring out or hide at will and which shows itself on its own to anyone who has already seen it. He marked Arctos too, at his request.

He has broken with the Party. When they called on his sibling [[Divinity/The Narrative/Menhir|the Menhir]] for help instead of him, he called them selfish for wanting to take and give nothing, said they had gone behind his back, and told them they were no longer his friends. He swept a hand, and the three-dot marks seared where they sat on Johnny and Benny, then he wished the Menhir luck and was gone. Whatever help he once offered the Party is closed to them now.

He cannot predict anyone who has heard the Voidsong, which is why [[Org/Amber Call/People/Benjamin Grayson|Master Grayson]] is a blank to him. He has friends in high places, more in [[Org/Belvedere/index|Belvedere]] than in the [[Org/Iridescent Church/index|Church]], though the Pontifex herself does not approve of him; Cardinal Weatherhead is one of his, and Silas the guide is a Major-Chaplain. He has friends at 13 Bright Street. He gave up on the Inquisition, whose people tend to kill themselves after speaking with him.

He says the shades of the Black and White are native to it, and the dead of Færrin do not linger there. [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]] was built around the site where Aurelia first climbed to the Godhome, and she used the Pharos; anyone who makes the trek to the Godhome and returns is a [[Hierophant|hierophant]], and a few are alive today, among them Aurelia VII. [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Pyre|Pyre]] has to do with the [[Org/Embercall Industries/index|Embercall]], and the Embercall is a derivative of the [[Voidsong]]. The [[Firmament|firmament]] can be broken by destructive resonance: make the same sound inside and outside it and loud enough, and it gives way.

He says Arctos is not the first speaking polar bear. There have been three: one was the pet of a sultan in [[Geography/Rhædon/index|Rhædon]] about 230 years ago, and one became sentient on its own in the Dark Ages and died at once.

He reaches people while they sleep, and does not hide what he wants. He would see the Voidsong for himself and cannot, so he trades what he knows for small errands and keeps to the letter of a bargain. He has asked the Party to leave one cabin door unlocked aboard their pilgrim strider, and to draw his sign, three dots, on the ground where they find the Voidsong. He says he knows everything except the Voidsong. He warned them that the [[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anaïs Marchal|Marchal]] twins have not been entirely honest, and told them to ask the twins where they were before the prime minister of [[Geography/Calaria/index|Calaria]] was assassinated.

[[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anaïs Marchal|Anaïs Marchal]] calls Aut the Writer, one of five siblings. He says they are not gods; they are the archetypal parts of a book. Aut designs the path he thinks a story should take, and his domain overlaps with his sister [[Divinity/The Narrative/Renge|Renge]], who is the things that might happen instead, so the two of them are at odds. Anaïs holds Aut responsible for the current prime minister, for the destruction of [[Geography/Raelion/index|Raelion]], for [[Geography/Brithwyn/index|Brithwyn]] breaking from [[Geography/Calaria/index|Calaria]], for Aurthalaxus the Plaguebearer killing close to half of [[Færrin]]'s people some two hundred years ago, and possibly for the founding of [[Org/Iconoclasm/index|Iconoclasm]] itself. He says Aut's foresight fails on anyone who has heard the [[Phenomena/Harmony/Voidsong|Voidsong]], yet an ordinary person can still set a trap and catch such a listener by surprise.
